NETWORK ENGINEERING REPORT: CCNA COMPANY PROJECT

- What it is: a three-floor corporate campus network for six departments.
- Scale: 40 clients (19 PCs, 11 laptops, 6 smartphones, 4 printers), 2 servers, 6 access points.
- Internet: two core routers connected to two ISP routers in a full mesh.
- Resilience goal: no single failure in switching, routing or internet access stops the service.
- Two accepted exceptions: DHCP and the servers are single instances; NAT works only on the primary ISP path.

Key design decisions and why:
1. Hierarchical design with a collapsed core. Only six access switches exist, so a separate core layer would add devices with no benefit.
2. One VLAN and one subnet per department. Isolates broadcasts, allows per-department policy, simplifies moves.
3. Inter-VLAN routing on multilayer switches (SVIs). Hardware routing, and no single trunk cable acting as a bottleneck (the weakness of Router-on-a-Stick).
4. HSRP per VLAN, with the STP root on the same switch. Layer 2 and Layer 3 paths coincide, so there is no extra hop over the inter-switch link.
5. Routed /30 links between multilayer switches and core routers. No STP dependency at the edge, faster failover, smaller broadcast domains.
6. OSPF, single area. Open standard, fast convergence, enough for this size.
7. Two edge routers and two ISPs in full mesh. Any one router or ISP can fail.
8. VLSM addressing: /25 for departments, /28 for the server room, /30 for links. No wasted addresses.
9. Layered security: encrypted secrets, SSH only, port security, unused ports shut, WPA2.

Numbers: 3 floors, 6 departments, 6 access switches (2960-24TT), 2 multilayer switches (3650-24PS), 2 core routers (2911), 2 ISP routers (2811), 6 access points, 2 servers, 14 subnets (6 departments, 4 internal links, 4 ISP links).

2. OBJECTIVES, SCOPE, ASSUMPTIONS

Objectives:
1. Full connectivity between departments, with broadcast isolation.
2. Internet access for everyone through NAT overload.
3. Survive the loss of one multilayer switch, one core router, one ISP or one uplink.
4. Company services: website, DNS, email, FTP (cloud storage).
5. One access point and one SSID per department.
6. DHCP for clients; static addresses for servers and printers.
7. Basic security on every device and port.
8. Easy maintenance: consistent naming, fixed addressing pattern, documentation.

Assumptions:
- Packet Tracer 9.0.1 simplifies or lacks some real features.
- 195.136.17.0/28 is lab space for the ISP links.
- Passwords follow a lab pattern (name + 123.0) and must change in production.
- DHCP runs on Multi-SW1 only.

Out of scope: BGP, IPv6, VoIP, firewalls, ACLs between departments.

3. DESIGN PRINCIPLES

3.1 Hierarchy
- Access: connects users, VLANs, port security. Needs many cheap ports.
- Aggregation/core: fast routing and redundancy.
- Edge: external connectivity and NAT.
- Benefits: a failure stays inside its layer; adding a department needs one switch and one VLAN; the layer tells you where to troubleshoot.

3.2 Why a collapsed core
- Merging distribution and core gives one pair of devices instead of two: fewer cables and configs.
- Split it when access switches exceed about 10 to 12, a second building is added, or ACLs and QoS need separating from fast forwarding.

3.3 No single point of failure
- Access to aggregation: each access switch has an uplink to both multilayer switches.
- Default gateway: HSRP per VLAN.
- Aggregation to edge: four routed links, every switch to every router.
- Edge to internet: four serial links plus a floating static route.
- Not redundant (accepted): the access switch of each department, DHCP, the servers.

3.4 Simplicity: one routing protocol, one area, PVST+ instead of MST, one addressing pattern.
3.5 Defense in depth: management, port, network and wireless controls, so one failure leaves the others.
3.6 Consistency: same address pattern in every subnet, VLAN ID equals HSRP group, one naming and password rule.

4. ARCHITECTURE AND DEVICE SELECTION

4.1 Devices
- Access: Sales-SW, HR-SW, FIN-SW, ICT-SW, Admin-SW, Server-SW (2960-24TT). Many cheap ports, no Layer 3 needed.
- Aggregation and core: Multi-SW1, Multi-SW2 (3650-24PS). Supports ip routing, SVIs, Gigabit.
- Edge: CORE-1, CORE-2 (2911). Gigabit ports and a HWIC slot for serial ports.
- Provider: ISP-1, ISP-2 (2811). Simulation only (loopbacks 8.8.8.8 and 9.9.9.9).

4.2 Why a multilayer switch, not Router-on-a-Stick
- Router-on-a-Stick sends all inter-VLAN traffic in and out of one router over one trunk: bottleneck and single point of failure.
- A multilayer switch routes between SVIs internally in hardware, needs no extra cable, and two switches give redundancy through HSRP.

4.3 Why separate edge routers
- If one pair did both internal routing and edge, an internet fault could break internal routing.
- With separation, losing a core router does not touch internal routing, and losing a multilayer switch does not cut the internet (both routers connect to both switches).

4.4 Uplink oversubscription
1. Access uplinks are FastEthernet (100 Mbps); STP leaves one forwarding.
2. The busiest switch is FIN-SW: 7 wired devices plus an access point.
3. Worst case: 8 x 100 Mbps = 800 Mbps offered to 100 Mbps, ratio 8 to 1.
4. Acceptable because office traffic is bursty.
5. If congestion appears: Gigabit uplinks or EtherChannel.

4.5 Cabling summary
- Every access switch: Fa0/1 to Multi-SW1, Fa0/2 to Multi-SW2.
- Sales-SW Fa0/1 to Multi-SW1 Gi1/0/3, Fa0/2 to Multi-SW2 Gi1/0/9. HR-SW: Gi1/0/4 and Gi1/0/7. FIN-SW: Gi1/0/5 and Gi1/0/6. ICT-SW: Gi1/0/6 and Gi1/0/5. Admin-SW: Gi1/0/7 and Gi1/0/4. Server-SW: Gi1/0/8 and Gi1/0/3.
- Inter-switch link: Multi-SW1 Gi1/0/10 to Multi-SW2 Gi1/0/8.

5. LAYER 2 DESIGN

5.1 VLANs
Why one VLAN per department:
- Broadcast isolation: ARP and DHCP stay inside the department.
- Security: departments talk only through a routing point where ACLs can be added.
- Management: moving an employee means changing a port VLAN, not recabling.
- Performance: each /25 holds at most 126 hosts.

Numbering: 10 to 60 in steps of 10 to leave room for new VLANs. VLAN 1 is not used for users (default and a common attack target).
- VLAN 10 Sales (Sales and Marketing) 172.16.1.0/25
- VLAN 20 HR (HR and Logistics) 172.16.1.128/25
- VLAN 30 Finance (Finance and Accounts) 172.16.2.0/25
- VLAN 40 ICT 172.16.3.0/25
- VLAN 50 Admin (Admin and Public Relations) 172.16.2.128/25
- VLAN 60 Server (Server Room) 172.16.3.128/28

VLAN database: each access switch defines only its own VLAN, so it neither forwards nor runs STP for VLANs it does not need. Both multilayer switches define all six.
Management: each access switch has a management SVI in its own VLAN (for example 172.16.1.2 on Sales-SW). A dedicated management VLAN is better practice and is listed in the recommendations.

5.2 Trunks
- 802.1Q: open standard; ISL is obsolete.
- Static trunk mode (switchport mode trunk): dynamic mode uses DTP, which lets an unknown device negotiate a trunk (basis of VLAN hopping).
- Native VLAN 1 and all VLANs allowed: defaults kept for simplicity; hardening is in the recommendations.
- Where: Fa0/1 and Fa0/2 on access switches; Gi1/0/3 to Gi1/0/10 on the multilayer switches.

5.3 Spanning Tree
- Why STP: dual uplinks create loops; without STP broadcasts circulate forever (no TTL in Ethernet) and the network collapses.
- Why PVST+: Cisco default, one instance per VLAN; MST is unnecessary for six VLANs.
- Root election: lowest priority wins (default 32768). Left to chance, an old access switch could become root.
- Multi-SW1: priority 24576 (root primary). Multi-SW2: 28672 (root secondary). Access switches: default. Values are multiples of 4096 because the VLAN ID takes 12 bits.

Why Multi-SW1 is the root:
1. It is also the HSRP Active gateway for all VLANs.
2. If the root were Multi-SW2, frames would go access switch, Multi-SW2, inter-switch link, Multi-SW1.
3. With both roles on one switch the path is direct.
4. This saves a hop and keeps load off the inter-switch link.

Which ports are blocked:
1. On each access switch Fa0/1 is the root port (cost 19 straight to the root).
2. Fa0/2 reaches the root via Multi-SW2 (19 + 4), so it is the alternate port in Blocking.
3. If Multi-SW1 or Fa0/1 fails, Fa0/2 moves to Forwarding.

Convergence: about 30 seconds in PVST+ (up to about 50 s for indirect failures). Rapid-PVST (1 to 2 s) is recommended for production.
Accepted trade-offs: half of the uplinks idle (no per-VLAN load balancing, kept for simplicity); one inter-switch cable (an LACP EtherChannel would be better).

5.4 Port security
Threats: an unauthorised device on a wall port; MAC flooding (CAM overflow).
Settings on every used endpoint port:
- switchport mode access: one end device, no trunk negotiation.
- mac-address sticky: learns the MAC and saves it in the config.
- maximum 1: one device per port.
- maximum 5 on access-point ports: up to 4 wireless clients (Sales, FIN) plus the access point.
- violation shutdown (default): strongest response (err-disabled).
Modes compared: protect drops silently; restrict drops and logs; shutdown disables the port and logs.
Operating steps:
1. After the build, ping from every device so sticky MACs are learned.
2. Run write memory to save them.
3. To recover a port: fix the cause, then shutdown and no shutdown.
4. When replacing a device: clear port-security sticky interface <port>.
Unused ports are shut down on every access switch. Port security is not applied on trunks and routed ports.

6. LAYER 3 DESIGN

6.1 Inter-VLAN routing
1. Each VLAN has an SVI on both multilayer switches.
2. ip routing makes each SVI network a connected route.
3. A packet between departments is routed inside the active multilayer switch and never reaches the core routers.
4. Why: shortest path; internal traffic is independent of the internet edge.

6.2 HSRP
Why: a PC has one gateway address; if that were a real device and it failed, the PC would lose off-subnet access. HSRP gives both switches one virtual IP and MAC.
Design:
- One group per VLAN, group number equals VLAN ID.
- Multi-SW1: priority 110 with preempt (Active). Multi-SW2: priority 100 (Standby).
- Preempt returns the Active role to Multi-SW1 after it recovers, so traffic goes back to the optimal path.
- Hello 3 s, hold 10 s: failover in about 10 seconds.
Addresses (virtual IP, Multi-SW1 SVI, Multi-SW2 SVI):
- VLAN 10: 172.16.1.1, .3, .4
- VLAN 20: 172.16.1.129, .131, .132
- VLAN 30: 172.16.2.1, .3, .4
- VLAN 40: 172.16.3.1, .3, .4
- VLAN 50: 172.16.2.129, .131, .132
- VLAN 60: 172.16.3.129, .131, .132
When Multi-SW1 fails:
1. Multi-SW2 stops hearing hellos.
2. After about 10 s it becomes Active for all six groups.
3. Clients keep the same gateway IP.
4. On recovery, preempt returns the Active role to Multi-SW1.

6.3 Routed links (multilayer switches to core routers)
Why routed ports, not trunks: no STP dependency, no blocked links, faster failover; each link is its own small broadcast domain; a /30 wastes no addresses.
- Multi-SW1 Gi1/0/1 to CORE-1 Gi0/0: 172.16.3.144/30 (.145 / .146)
- Multi-SW2 Gi1/0/1 to CORE-1 Gi0/1: 172.16.3.148/30 (.149 / .150)
- Multi-SW1 Gi1/0/2 to CORE-2 Gi0/0: 172.16.3.152/30 (.153 / .154)
- Multi-SW2 Gi1/0/2 to CORE-2 Gi0/1: 172.16.3.156/30 (.157 / .158)

6.4 OSPF
Why OSPF: open standard (EIGRP is Cisco-only); link-state, fast and loop-free (RIP is slow, 15 hops max); static routes cannot react to failures. Single area: only four routing devices.
Configuration and reasons:
1. network 172.16.0.0 0.0.255.255 area 0 on Multi-SW1, Multi-SW2, CORE-1, CORE-2: one statement covers all internal subnets and links.
2. Router IDs: CORE-1 1.1.1.1, CORE-2 2.2.2.2, Multi-SW1 3.3.3.3, Multi-SW2 4.4.4.4: stable identity.
3. passive-interface default on the multilayer switches, with Gi1/0/1 and Gi1/0/2 active: no hellos into user VLANs, nobody can form an adjacency from a user port.
4. default-information originate on both core routers: they advertise the internet default route.
Expected: two FULL neighbours on each multilayer switch; two equal-cost default routes (ECMP). On Ethernet, OSPF elects a DR and BDR (point-to-point network type would remove that; a refinement).

6.5 Default routes and WAN failover
- CORE-1: primary 0.0.0.0/0 via 195.136.17.2 (ISP-1); backup via 195.136.17.6 (ISP-2), distance 10.
- CORE-2: primary via 195.136.17.10 (ISP-1); backup via 195.136.17.14 (ISP-2), distance 10.
- Why static: no real ISP to run BGP with; an edge router only needs a default.
- How failover works: the backup has distance 10 so it stays hidden; if the primary serial link goes down the primary route is removed and the backup enters the table.
- Limitation: NAT is bound to the ISP-1 link, so the backup path does not translate.

6.6 NAT overload
1. ACL 1 permits 172.16.0.0 0.0.255.255.
2. Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 are ip nat inside; Se0/0/0 and Se0/0/1 are ip nat outside (both core routers).
3. CORE-1: ip nat inside source list 1 interface Serial0/0/0 overload.
4. CORE-2: ip nat inside source list 1 interface Serial0/0/1 overload.
- Why NAT: 172.16.0.0/16 is private and not routable on the internet.
- Why overload: many hosts share one outside address using port numbers.
- Limitation: on the backup path packets leave untranslated and the ISP drops the replies.
- Improvement: one NAT statement per ISP using route-maps, or IP SLA tracking.

6.7 ISP simulation
- ISP-1 and ISP-2 have only connected networks and a loopback each (8.8.8.8 and 9.9.9.9).
- They need no inside routes because NAT hides the inside addresses.
- The DCE side of each serial link carries clock rate 2000000.

7. IP ADDRESSING AND SUBNETTING

7.1 Why 172.16.0.0
- 172.16.0.0/12 is private (RFC 1918), larger than 192.168.0.0/16 and less likely to clash with home networks or VPNs.
- Only 172.16.1.0 to 172.16.3.255 is used, so there is plenty of room to grow.

7.2 Subnetting steps
1. Count devices per department (clients, access point, printers, infrastructure addresses).
2. Pick the smallest prefix that fits with growth room (usable hosts = 2^n - 2).
3. Allocate from one base block in increasing order.
4. Reserve fixed addresses at the start of each subnet.
Devices today: Sales 13, HR 12, Finance 15, ICT 11, Admin 10, Server Room 11.
- /25 gives 2^7 - 2 = 126 hosts (all five departments).
- Server Room: 11 needed; /29 gives 6 (too small); /28 gives 14.
- /30 gives 2 (point-to-point links).
- Bonus: all internal addresses are inside 172.16.0.0/22, so one summary route covers the campus.

7.3 Department subnets (network, mask, usable range, broadcast)
- Sales 172.16.1.0/25, 255.255.255.128, .1 to .126, 172.16.1.127
- HR 172.16.1.128/25, 255.255.255.128, .129 to .254, 172.16.1.255
- Finance 172.16.2.0/25, 255.255.255.128, .1 to .126, 172.16.2.127
- Admin 172.16.2.128/25, 255.255.255.128, .129 to .254, 172.16.2.255
- ICT 172.16.3.0/25, 255.255.255.128, .1 to .126, 172.16.3.127
- Server Room 172.16.3.128/28, 255.255.255.240, .129 to .142, 172.16.3.143

7.4 Pattern inside every subnet
- First address: HSRP virtual IP. Second: access switch SVI. Third and fourth: Multi-SW1 and Multi-SW2.
- Then static devices, then the DHCP range.
- Why: anyone can predict any department's gateway and switch addresses.
- Why exclusions: DHCP must never give out an address used by infrastructure or a static device.
- Sales: DHCP excludes .1-.9, leases .10-.126.
- HR: Printer1 172.16.1.136, Printer2 172.16.1.137; excludes .129-.138, leases .139-.254.
- Finance: Printer0 172.16.2.5; excludes .1-.9, leases .10-.126.
- ICT: excludes .1-.9, leases .10-.126.
- Admin: Printer3 172.16.2.135; excludes .129-.138, leases .139-.254.
- Server Room: Server0 172.16.3.133, Server1 172.16.3.134; excludes .129-.134, leases .135-.142.

7.5 ISP links
- CORE-1 Se0/0/0 (.1, DCE) to ISP-1 Se0/2/0 (.2): 195.136.17.0/30
- CORE-1 Se0/0/1 (.5) to ISP-2 Se0/2/0 (.6, DCE): 195.136.17.4/30
- CORE-2 Se0/0/1 (.9) to ISP-1 Se0/2/1 (.10, DCE): 195.136.17.8/30
