8. SERVICES

8.1 DHCP (on Multi-SW1)
How a client gets an address:
1. The PC broadcasts a DHCP Discover on its VLAN.
2. The Multi-SW1 SVI in that VLAN receives it and picks the pool matching the SVI subnet.
3. The switch offers an address, skipping excluded ranges.
4. The PC requests it and the switch confirms.
5. The client also gets the gateway (HSRP virtual IP) and DNS 172.16.3.133.
- Why centralised: one place to manage; no helper address needed.
- Risk: if Multi-SW1 fails, new clients get no address; existing leases keep working until they expire.
- Fix: split ranges on Multi-SW2 or use a redundant DHCP server.
Pools: Sales, HR, FIN, ICT, Admin, Server, one per subnet.

8.2 Server room
- Servers sit in their own VLAN 60 so all access to them crosses a routing point where ACLs can apply.
- Static IPs keep DNS records, mail settings and the DHCP DNS option stable.

8.3 Server0: website and DNS (172.16.3.133)
- HTTP and HTTPS On, with the company page.
- DNS On: www.project.local = 172.16.3.133; mail.project.local = 172.16.3.134; cloud.project.local = 172.16.3.134.
- Why DNS: users type names; moving a service later only needs a record change.

8.4 Server1: email and cloud storage (172.16.3.134)
How a message travels:
1. The sender's client submits it to the server over SMTP.
2. The server stores it in the recipient mailbox.
3. The recipient's client collects it over POP3 when pressing Receive.
Mailboxes: sales, hr, fin, ict, admin at project.local, each with password name + 123.0.
Cloud storage: FTP user cloud (password cloud123.0, permissions read, write, delete, rename, list). The default cisco account should be removed in production.

8.5 Mail client settings (all PCs, laptops, smartphones of the five departments)
1. Desktop, then Email.
2. Your Name: the department name.
3. Email Address: user@project.local.
4. Incoming and outgoing server: 172.16.3.134.
5. User name: short user (for example sales); password: user password.
6. Server Room devices (PC18, PC19, Laptop9, Laptop10) have no mailbox.

8.6 Printers (static, inside the excluded ranges)
- Printer0 (FIN-SW Fa0/5): 172.16.2.5/25, gateway 172.16.2.1
- Printer1 (HR-SW Fa0/5): 172.16.1.136/25, gateway 172.16.1.129
- Printer2 (HR-SW Fa0/6): 172.16.1.137/25, gateway 172.16.1.129
- Printer3 (Admin-SW Fa0/5): 172.16.2.135/25, gateway 172.16.2.129
- Why static: every client reaches the printer at a fixed address and DHCP never reassigns it.

9. WIRELESS DESIGN

Principles:
1. One access point per department, plugged into an access port of that department's VLAN.
2. All clients of that SSID land in the right VLAN with no tagging on the access point.
3. Each department has its own SSID, so a device associates only with its own department.
4. Channels 1, 6, 11 are the only non-overlapping 2.4 GHz channels (22 MHz wide, 5 MHz spacing); a channel is reused only on a different floor.
5. WPA2-PSK with AES: WEP and TKIP are broken or deprecated. Limit: one shared passphrase per department; WPA2-Enterprise with RADIUS would give per-user credentials.
Access points:
- Sales: Sales-SW Fa0/7, Sales-WiFi, channel 1, clients Laptop0-2 and Smartphone0
- HR: HR-SW Fa0/7, HR-WiFi, channel 6, clients Laptop3-5
- FIN: FIN-SW Fa0/9, FIN-WiFi, channel 11, clients Laptop6-7 and Smartphone1-2
- ICT: ICT-SW Fa0/7, ICT-WiFi, channel 1, clients Smartphone3-4
- Admin: Admin-SW Fa0/6, Admin-WiFi, channel 6, clients Laptop8 and Smartphone5
- Server: Server-SW Fa0/7, Server-WiFi, channel 11, clients Laptop9-10
Passphrase: department name + 123.0.
Client setup:
1. Laptops: power off, replace the copper module with WPC300N, power on.
2. Config, then Wireless0, enter the SSID.
3. Select WPA2-PSK with AES and the passphrase.
4. Set DHCP in Desktop, IP Configuration.
5. Smartphones use the built-in adapter; repeat steps 2 to 4.

10. SECURITY HARDENING

Baseline on every switch and router:
1. hostname: identifies the device.
2. no ip domain-lookup: stops the CLI freezing on a mistyped command (usability).
3. service password-encryption: hides line passwords from onlookers (type 7, reversible, weak).
4. enable secret: salted hash (type 5); enable password is clear text, so it is not used.
5. Console password and login: protects the physical port.
6. Banner: legal warning (text: only authorized access!!).
7. ip domain-name project.local and crypto key generate rsa general-keys modulus 1024: SSH needs both.
8. ip ssh version 2: avoids SSH v1 weaknesses.
9. username <name> secret <password>: local account with a hashed password.
10. line vty with login local and transport input ssh: SSH only, Telnet refused.
- Multilayer switches use line vty 0 15.
- On the ISP routers the delivered file shows service password-encryption disabled; enable it.
Where applied: secrets, SSH, banner on all 12 network devices; sticky port security and shut unused ports on the six access switches; static trunks everywhere; OSPF passive interfaces on the multilayer switches; WPA2 on the access points and wireless clients.

11. RESILIENCE ANALYSIS

1. Multi-SW1 fails: Multi-SW2 becomes HSRP Active after about 10 s; access switches unblock Fa0/2 after about 30 to 50 s; Multi-SW2 becomes STP root; new DHCP leases fail. Short interruption.
2. Multi-SW2 fails: the Active path is unaffected; only redundancy is lost.
3. CORE-1 fails: each multilayer switch loses one OSPF neighbour and keeps the default route through CORE-2, which reaches ISP-1 with NAT. Immediate on link-down, up to 40 s (OSPF dead timer) otherwise.
4. CORE-2 fails: the same, through CORE-1.
5. ISP-1 fails: the cores switch to the floating route via ISP-2, but NAT is bound to ISP-1, so internet traffic is not translated and fails. Internal traffic is unaffected. Fix: route-map NAT.
6. One access uplink fails: STP unblocks the other uplink in about 30 s.
7. Access switch fails: that department is isolated; the rest is unaffected.
8. Server fails: its services are unavailable (single instance).
9. Inter-switch link fails: the two multilayer switches lose their direct path; a second link in an EtherChannel is recommended.

12. TRAFFIC FLOWS

A. Sales PC to HR PC
1. The PC ARPs for its gateway 172.16.1.1 (HSRP virtual MAC) and sends the frame.
2. Sales-SW forwards it on the Fa0/1 trunk, tagged VLAN 10, to Multi-SW1.
3. Multi-SW1 (HSRP Active) routes it from VLAN 10 to VLAN 20 internally.
4. It sends the frame out the trunk toward HR-SW, tagged VLAN 20.
5. HR-SW delivers it to the PC port. The reply follows the same path back.
B. PC to internet (8.8.8.8)
1. The PC sends to its gateway; Multi-SW1 looks up the default route (OSPF).
2. It forwards to CORE-1 or CORE-2 (equal cost).
3. The core router translates the source address (NAT overload) and sends it over the serial link to ISP-1.
4. ISP-1 answers from loopback 8.8.8.8; the core router translates the reply back.
C. Web by name
1. The client asks DNS (172.16.3.133) for www.project.local and gets 172.16.3.133.
2. It opens HTTP to that address across VLANs through Multi-SW1.
D. Email
1. The client sends SMTP to 172.16.3.134.
2. The server stores the message; the recipient presses Receive and collects it over POP3.

