1. Admin-SW ports were in VLAN 60 instead of 50, with an extra Vlan60 SVI: moved to VLAN 50 and removed the SVI.
2. NAT was missing on both core routers: added ACL 1, inside/outside, overload.
3. Three of the four serial links had mismatched addresses (wiring did not match the plan): swapped addresses on CORE-1 and ISP-1.
4. The ISPs had no loopbacks, so internet tests were impossible: added 8.8.8.8 and 9.9.9.9.
5. Sales-SW second uplink was on Multi-SW1 Gi1/0/9 instead of Multi-SW2: moved, restoring redundancy.
6. Commands were pasted in the wrong CLI mode (DHCP pools in privileged EXEC, IPs before no switchport, OSPF before ip routing): redone in the right mode.
7. enable password and username password on Multi-SW1, extra password on Sales-SW: replaced by secret.
8. Trunk was missing on Multi-SW1 Gi1/0/9-10.
9. Laptops had wired cards: replaced with WPC300N.
10. Startup config differed from running config: saved with write memory.
11. Printer3 had no address and email clients were unconfigured: fixed.
12. First ping loss between departments is normal ARP behaviour, not a fault.

15. RECOMMENDATIONS

1. ACLs: allow departments to reach only the servers (HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, SMTP, POP3, FTP); block Sales to Admin.
2. DHCP redundancy: split ranges on Multi-SW2 or use a redundant server.
3. NAT per ISP using route-maps or IP SLA tracking.
4. Rapid-PVST, PortFast and BPDU Guard on access ports.
5. EtherChannel (LACP) between the multilayer switches.
6. Per-VLAN load balancing: root and HSRP Active alternate between the two switches.
7. Management VLAN, native VLAN changed to an unused one, allowed VLANs pruned.
8. DHCP snooping and dynamic ARP inspection.
9. HSRP authentication and interface tracking.
10. OSPF point-to-point network type on the /30 links; summary route 172.16.0.0/22.
11. WPA2-Enterprise with RADIUS and more access points per department.
12. Centralised logging and time sync (syslog, NTP); remove the default cisco account on the servers.

16. CONCLUSION

The network meets its objectives: department isolation, full internal connectivity, redundant gateways and routing, internet access with failover, company services and wireless access. Its two known weaknesses (single DHCP and server instances, NAT only on the primary ISP) are documented with fixes in section 15. Security is basic but consistent across all devices.
